  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}+7x$ at the point $(1,9).$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}+7x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}+7x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-5}+7x) \\\\  & =-10{x}^{-6}+7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{\u2032}(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(1)=-10(1{)}^{-6}+7=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-3$ and the coordinates of our point $(1,9),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(9) & =-3(x-1) \\\\ y & =-3x+3+9 \\\\ y & =-3x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
